diff --git a/docs/DESIGN.md b/docs/DESIGN.md
deleted file mode 100644
index 1a08c91619fedc28bed1e2192627e9af0c9bc78b..0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
--- a/docs/DESIGN.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,354 +0,0 @@
-# Design
-
-This document records the interface design of the Phial application and the reason
-for each decision. The rules come from the GNOME Human Interface Guidelines.
-
-## The window
-
-The window holds one sidebar and one content area.
-
-```
-AdwApplicationWindow
-└── AdwToastOverlay
-    └── AdwNavigationSplitView          collapsed below 550sp
-        ├── sidebar  AdwNavigationPage
-        │   └── AdwToolbarView
-        │       ├── AdwHeaderBar        New Machine, Main Menu
-        │       └── AdwSidebar          Machines, Secrets, Grants
-        └── content  AdwNavigationPage
-            └── AdwNavigationView       one page per machine, and the Secrets and
-                                        Grants pages
-```
-
-The sidebar holds the machine list, because a user switches between machines often.
-The navigation guideline allows a sidebar for dynamic locations and for frequent
-switching.
-
-The breakpoint at 550sp collapses the split view. The sidebar then becomes the first
-page of the stack and the content area follows it. The header bar of the content
-area carries the back control.
-
-A page switch names the pages of the new stack, and the widget removes every other
-page of the view. The application adds the cached pages back before each switch, so
-that a later switch finds them.
-
-## The sidebar
-
-The sidebar carries one section for the machines and one section for the tools.
-
-- A machine row carries the hard disk icon, the name, and the state as a subtitle.
-- The Secrets row carries the password icon and a quiet pill with the number of
-  secrets and keys.
-- The Grants row carries the alarm icon, a quiet pill with the number of open
-  grants, and an accent pill with the number of waiting requests.
-
-The New Machine control and the main menu sit in the header bar of the sidebar. The
-navigation guideline asks for sidebar controls above the list and for the main menu
-above the sidebar list.
-
-## The empty state
-
-A fresh installation carries no machine, and the window states that once.
-
-- The Machines section keeps its heading and carries one row, Get Started.
-- The row is selected, and it shows the empty page. The button on the page creates the machine.
-- The header shows no create control while the row is present, because the page carries the button.
-- The row opens no dialog, so the only control that creates a machine is the button.
-- The page carries the icon, the heading No Machines, one line of description, and one suggested pill button, New Machine.
-- The row leaves as soon as the first machine exists, and the header control returns with it.
-
-## The machine page
-
-The machine page is a settings style page with boxed lists. The order follows the
-questions that a user asks.
-
-1. **Installation** appears only while an install runs. It lists the seven install
-   steps and Cancel Installation. The current step carries the precise progress in its
-   subtitle, such as the downloaded size and the share of the download, or the package
-   name with its position in the package list.
-2. **Overview** carries the state and, while the machine runs, the live CPU, memory,
-   disk, network, and graphics figures. The graphics row states the busy share of the
-   render engine of the host. The disk row states the read and the write rates.
-3. **Applications and Desktop** carries Guest Applications, Remote Desktop, Terminal,
-   Serial Console, and the SSH command. Each row opens a dialog or copies a command.
-4. **Shared Folders** lists the shared host directories and offers Share a Folder.
-5. **USB Devices** lists the passed devices and offers Add USB Device. The chooser
-   hides the devices that any machine already forwards. A passed device that is not
-   attached right now states itself as disconnected.
-6. **USB Hubs** lists the passed controllers and offers Add USB Hub. The chooser
-   hides the controllers that any machine already uses.
-7. **Storage** carries the facts that no dialog changes: the disk image path, and a
-   control that shows the folder of the image in the file manager.
-
-Every value that a dialog can change lives in Machine Settings, and the page does not
-repeat it. The header bar carries one primary control, one pencil, and one menu:
-
-- The primary control is Install, Start, or Stop, and it follows the state of the machine.
-- The pencil opens Machine Settings.
-- The menu carries Logs, Reprovision the Guest Agent, Restart, and Delete Machine.
-- Logs opens the log of the machine in the text editor: the console of the installer when it keeps one, else the service log of the machine.
-- The Serial Console and Remote Desktop dialogs state the user and the password with a copy control for each, because the guest asks for both at its login.
-- The Remote Desktop dialog carries the `rdp://` link of the machine. It opens the link in the default handler of the desktop.
-- The link uses `localhost`, which resolves to `::1` first. The forward listens on the IPv6 loopback only.
-- The Terminal dialog carries Unlock the SSH Agent, which loads the key of the user into the agent of the session and asks for the passphrase through pinentry.
-- Every command in the guest states a timeout, so a guest that stops answering states an error instead of holding the app.
-- The install provisions the machine: it boots the guest once, waits for the guest agent to answer, copies the units of the guest into it, and stops it again.
-- The guest agent is the binary of Phial itself, so the reprovision step, which runs the provisioning again, replaces the agent of a machine as well.
-- The provisioning of a machine is idempotent, so the reprovision item in the menu of a machine runs it at any time.
-- The provisioning travels over the channel of the QEMU guest agent, which is a virtio serial port. The vsock carries the shell of the guest instead.
-- The QEMU guest agent of the guest runs in a confined SELinux domain, so the provisioning sends the script and the binary to /run/phial through the file API of the agent, and a path unit of the guest starts the service that runs the script as root without confinement.
-- The provisioning writes its own service and path units, so a machine that an older installer set up converges on the same state as a new install.
-- The page compares the build time of the host with the build time that the provisioning wrote into the guest, and asks for a reprovision with a banner when the guest is behind. The check reads /etc over the guest agent, because the confined domain reads nothing of the home of the user.
-- The guest turns plymouth off and masks the network wait, because a machine that waits for a splash or for the network never reaches a login.
-- The installer writes the home directory of the guest as root, so the provisioning repairs the labels of that directory before the session of the user writes its units.
-- A machine installs in minutes rather than seconds, so the first start of a fresh machine needs a few minutes before the guest answers.
-- The renderer of the host fails the DRM native context of Venus only when the guest initializes the GPU, so no probe can state it. A machine with such a renderer restarts once without the option and keeps that choice in its definition.
-- The stop of a machine asks the guest to shut down through the guest agent and presses the power button when no agent answers, so a stop flushes the writes of the guest; a guest that ignores the shutdown is switched off after a timeout.
-- The generated ssh configuration names no agent, so ssh uses the agent of the session, which asks for the passphrase of the key on the first use.
-
-Rules that the page keeps:
-
-- The page never shows an impossible control. Cancel Installation replaces the
-  primary control while an install runs, and the delete control stays insensitive.
-- The access rows become insensitive while the machine is stopped, because no entry
-  can reach a machine that does not run.
-- The page shows the disk path and the configuration as property rows, because a
-  property row carries a name and a value and it reflows at any width.
-
-## The Secrets page
-
-The Secrets page lists what a machine can be granted, in three groups. The
-private parts never leave the host. The header bar states the rule in one
-sentence, and the groups carry no description of their own.
-
-- The Tokens group lists the values of the host keyring, one row per token. A row
-  carries the name, the mode, and the machines that hold a grant on it. The page
-  never shows a value. The group carries the name Tokens, because the page
-  already carries the name Secrets.
-- A row opens the edit dialog. An empty value field keeps the stored value.
-  Add Token stores a value in the Secret Service of the session. Delete removes
-  the value, the row, and every grant on the token.
-- The SSH Keys group lists the keys of the session agent: the comment, the
-  fingerprint, and the type. Add SSH Key loads a key file into the agent, and
-  Unload drops it again. The file on disk stays.
-- The GnuPG Keys group lists the secret keys of the host agent: the uid and the
-  short fingerprint. Add GnuPG Key imports a key file into the keyring, and
-  Delete removes the secret part only, so old signatures still verify. A GnuPG
-  key carries no unload, because the agent serves it from the keyring itself and
-  holds no second copy.
-- Every list ends with its own actions, because a reader who adds one kind of
-  entry reads that list. Add Token sits below the tokens, and Add SSH Key and
-  Generate SSH Key sit below the SSH keys.
-- Generate SSH Key makes a key pair in ~/.ssh and loads it into the agent.
-  Generate GnuPG Key makes a key in the host keyring. The GnuPG agent asks for
-  the passphrase through the pinentry of the host.
-- A host with no token and no key still shows the three lists, so that the
-  actions of each list stay reachable.
-- Every row carries one grant control: the insert-link icon with the tooltip
-  Grant Access, which opens the grant dialog for that entry. The control is the
-  same on every row and on every kind, and it never looks like the check icon of
-  Approve or the close icon of Revoke on the Grants page.
-- The grant control and the menu of a row are icon buttons without a frame, so
-  that the suffix of a row reads as one plain strip.
-
-## The Grants page
-
-A grant opens one target for one machine. The kind of the target comes from its
-source: a bare name is a secret, agent:// is a key of the session agent, and
-gpg-agent:// is the GnuPG agent of the host. One name can never mean two kinds,
-so the grant dialog asks for the machine and the duration only.
-
-- The Pending group lists every request that waits for an answer: a grant, or a
-  change to the secrets or the keys of the host. The row carries the action, the
-  machine, and the age, with Approve and Deny on the row.
-- The Active group lists every open grant: the name, the kind, the machine, and
-  the remaining time, with a revoke control on the row.
-- A duration is a time box, one use, or until revoked. A once grant closes after
-  the first signature or the first proxied request. An env secret cannot take a
-  once grant, because the host never sees the read.
-- The grant dialog defaults to a machine that runs.
-
-## The gate
-
-The gate is one user service, phial-gate.service, that runs phial gate. It
-listens on three vsock ports, and every port enforces the grants on its own:
-
-- Port 5551 answers the SSH agent protocol. List returns only the keys that the
-  grants of the calling machine open. Sign asks the user with a host prompt
-  before it forwards to the agent of the session, once per key per minute.
-- Port 5552 carries the GnuPG agent protocol. A connection needs an open gpg
-  grant for the calling machine, and the user approves each connection with a
-  host prompt. The agent of the host still asks for the passphrase through its
-  own pinentry.
-- Port 4000 is the secret proxy. The path of a request names one secret, and the
-  gate answers only with an open grant for the calling machine. The gate injects
-  the Authorization header and rewrites the Host header to the upstream.
-
-The vsock address of a connection names the machine, so a guest cannot claim to
-be another machine. A connection from an address that no machine owns serves
-nothing. The local socket of the gate belongs to the host itself and sees every
-grant; it exists for the tests.
-
-The transports stay open: a guest always finds the gate listening, and a grant
-decides what the gate serves. A machine mounts only its own secrets directory,
-so an env file written for one machine never reaches another.
-
-A request also raises a notification with a Review action. The notification
-carries the user to the Grants page. A request that nobody answers within five
-minutes counts as denied.
-
-## States and long-running tasks
-
-Every state of a machine and every task that takes time has a control, a message,
-and an end. The application never shows a state that it cannot leave.
-
-### A machine
-
-| State | The page shows | The header control |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Not installed | State "Not installed", no live usage | Install |
-| Installing | The step list with the progress of the current step, Cancel Installation | A spinner, no control, and an insensitive pencil |
-| Installed and stopped | State "Stopped", no live usage | Start |
-| Starting | State "Starting", a spinner in the header | Start, insensitive |
-| Running, guest not up | State "Starting", the usage rows | Stop |
-| Running and ready | State "Running", the usage rows, the access rows on | Stop |
-| Stopping | State "Stopping", a spinner in the header | Stop, insensitive |
-| Restarting | State "Restarting" | The control, insensitive |
-
-A transition clears when the machine reaches the new state, or after three minutes.
-The header control stays insensitive while a transition runs, so a second press
-cannot start a second transition. A stop ends only when the unit of the machine no
-longer runs, because the guest still shuts down while the unit deactivates.
-
-### The installation
-
-The installation carries seven steps. Each step carries one of four appearances:
-done with a checkmark, current with a spinner, pending and dimmed, or failed with the
-error as its subtitle.
-
-The current step carries the progress, so the page needs no second control:
-
-- The download step states the downloaded size, the full size, and the share, such as "381.0 MiB of 1.2 GiB, 32%".
-- The package step states the package name and its position, such as "kernel-core, 96 of 412 packages".
-- Every other step states the message of the installer.
-
-Cancel Installation asks for confirmation and stops the installer. A failed step keeps
-the error on screen, and the step states the reason that the virtual machine gave,
-such as a failed memory mapping.
-
-### The network
-
-A machine reaches the network through one of two paths, and both must work.
-
-- The LAN path uses the bridge and its tap device, and Phial adds the tap only when the bridge carries a link. A network device without a link stops the guest network from coming up at all, so the automatic setting leaves it out on a Wi-Fi host.
-- The fallback path runs passt, which serves DHCP, the resolver 192.168.100.1, and the traffic of the guest.
-- passt forwards the queries of the guest to the resolver of the host. It reaches the stub resolver of systemd-resolved at 127.0.0.53 when it exists, because the stub also handles the translation that an IPv6-only host needs.
-
-### The installer
-
-The installer runs on its own devices, so the settings of the machine cannot stop it.
-
-- The installer uses one console, the serial console, so its text interface and its messages reach the console log that Phial reads.
-- The installer uses a plain display device, because the accelerated devices can stop the guest kernel.
-- The installer turns the graphical boot screen off, because the boot otherwise waits for a screen that never appears.
-- The host network settings reach the installer through the kickstart file, such as the address of the resolver.
-
-### The host resources
-
-The host may not be able to serve what a machine asks for, so Phial states the
-facts and keeps the machine usable:
-
-- Machine Settings states the huge pages of the host, such as "12.5 GiB free of 16.5 GiB reserved, too little for this machine".
-- A machine whose memory does not fit the free huge pages runs on normal pages instead.
-- A machine whose memory does not fit the free memory runs unlocked instead, so it stays swappable.
-- Both steps are written to the log with the machine name and the size that was asked for.
-
-### The live usage
-
-The usage appears while the machine runs: CPU in cores, memory, the disk space on this
-host, the network rate, and the graphics mode. Each row carries a defined empty state:
-
-- The machine does not run: one dimmed row states that no live usage exists.
-- The guest agent does not answer: the network row states this.
-- No traffic: the network row states this.
-- The metrics fail: one dimmed row reports the failure.
-
-### The requests
-
-A request waits for an answer. The Grants page states the five minute limit in its
-empty state, and the sidebar badge counts the waiting requests.
-
-### The grants
-
-A grant is open or it is not. The Grants page counts down the time, and a grant
-that runs out closes on its own, because the expiry timer runs on the host.
-
-## Dialogs
-
-A dialog carries a short form or one decision. The application uses a dialog for
-these tasks:
-
-- Create a machine, edit a machine, add or edit a secret, generate a key, grant access, add a USB
-  device, and share a folder.
-- Show the guest applications, the remote desktop, the terminal, and the serial
-  console.
-- Report the system setup and the acceptance report.
-- Confirm a delete, a cancel, and a quit.
-
-A dialog carries the Cancel control first and the affirmative control last. The
-affirmative control stays insensitive until the form is valid. The Escape key closes
-the dialog.
-
-The header bar of a dialog names the action and carries no description. Its
-subtitle states context only, such as the name of a machine. The title names the
-action without an article: Add Token, Edit Token, Generate SSH Key, Generate
-GnuPG Key, Grant Access.
-
-A dialog with a Cancel control and an affirmative control carries no close
-button, because Cancel closes it. A dialog that only reports a fact keeps the
-close button.
-
-A group of a form names its object in one word: Token, SSH Key, GnuPG Key,
-Grant. A description of a group is one sentence. A combo row keeps its choices
-short, so that the choice fits the row and the list.
-
-A dialog that carries a list carries a search button at the start of its header bar
-and a refresh control at the end. The search bar slides out below the header bar, and
-typing into the dialog opens it as well. A query without matches states itself in the
-list.
-
-## Keyboard and accessibility
-
-- Ctrl+N creates a machine, a secret, or a key, Ctrl+Comma opens the
-  preferences, Ctrl+Question opens the shortcuts, Ctrl+W closes the window, and
-  Ctrl+Q quits.
-- Ctrl+1 to Ctrl+9 select the first nine machines.
-- Every control carries a label or an accessible tooltip. A label names the
-  action and carries no ellipsis, so that a row and a dialog look the same.
-- Every list row carries a title and, where it helps, a subtitle.
-- The interface carries no color-only information. A state is always spelled out.
-- Custom styling stays at two badge classes that use the accent color variables.
-
-## Adaptive behavior
-
-- The split view collapses below 550sp.
-- Every page sits inside an AdwClamp with a maximum size of 600, so that lines stay
-  short on a wide window.
-- The content area holds property rows and boxed lists. Both reflow at any width.
-- The window keeps its size and its last page between runs, and it restores both.
-
-## Terms in the interface
-
-One term carries one meaning in the interface, the documentation, and the code.
-
-- A machine runs one Fedora guest. The command line calls it a virtual machine.
-- A token is a value in the keyring of the host. The interface writes token, the
-  code and the command line write secret.
-- A key lives in the SSH agent of the session or in the GnuPG agent of the host.
-- A grant is a time box or a single use of one secret or key for one machine.
-- A request is an agent's ask for a change or a grant.
-- The gate is the service on the host that enforces the grants on every transport.
-- A shared folder is a host folder that the machine mounts. The interface writes shared folders, never shares.
-- A USB device is one device that a machine claims. A USB hub is the whole hub.
-- This host is the computer that runs Phial.
-- An agent is a program inside a machine that asks the host for access.
diff --git a/flatpak/README.md b/flatpak/README.md
deleted file mode 100644
index ef93e45b05a20575d08e1393339b19b7eb275603..0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
--- a/flatpak/README.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,88 +0,0 @@
-# Flatpak
-
-This directory carries the Flatpak manifest for Phial.
-
-## Build
-
-Install the builder:
-
-    sudo dnf install flatpak-builder
-
-Build the bundle:
-
-    make flatpak
-
-The bundle lands at flatpak/phial.flatpak. Install it with:
-
-    flatpak install --user flatpak/phial.flatpak
-
-## Modules
-
-The manifest builds only the tools that the sandbox executes. The list follows.
-
-- waypipe renders the windows of a guest application on the desktop of the host.
-- ovmf supplies the UEFI pair. The source is the Fedora edk2-ovmf RPM, which the
-  build unpacks with bsdtar. extra-data cannot carry this pair, because it
-  fetches single files at install time and cannot unpack an RPM.
-- Phial itself is the last module.
-
-waypipe tracks v0.11.2. The build turns off the optional lz4, zstd, gbm, and
-video wrappers, because their bindgen step needs libclang, which the SDK does
-not carry. No dependency of this app is in the Flathub shared-modules
-repository.
-
-The machine tools stay on the host. The units that the host install writes run
-swtpm, virtiofsd, passt, socat, and QEMU on the host, and QEMU reads the OVMF
-pair from the host. The RDP client stays outside the bundle as well. The window
-opens an `rdp://` link, and the handler of the desktop starts the client. A
-bundle copy of these tools cannot serve the host QEMU or the host client,
-because /app exists only inside the sandbox.
-
-## Permissions
-
-The manifest asks for the following and nothing more:
-
-- `--socket=wayland`, `--socket=fallback-x11`, `--share=ipc`, `--device=dri`
-  draw the window.
-- `--share=network` downloads the machine image, reaches the upstreams of
-  proxied secrets, and lists the local packages.
-- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.secrets` stores and reads the secrets in the
-  keyring of the session.
-- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.systemd1` starts and stops the user units that
-  run the machine, the gate, and the tools.
-- `--talk-name=org.freedesktop.Flatpak` runs the tools of the host through
-  flatpak-spawn, because ssh-add, gpg, run0, and pinentry live outside the
-  sandbox.
-- `--filesystem=~/.config/phial`, `--filesystem=~/.local/share/phial`, and
-  `--filesystem=xdg-run/phial` share the state of the host install.
-- `--filesystem=~/.ssh` reads the public keys that the session agent holds.
-
-Notifications, the background request, and the file dialogs go through the
-portals, so the manifest grants no direct access to the notification service.
-The root setup runs run0 on the host through flatpak-spawn, so the sandbox
-needs no PolicyKit talk.
-
-## Host requirements
-
-The host install writes user units that run the host copies of the machine
-tools. The host therefore needs:
-
-- qemu-system-x86_64 and edk2-ovmf for the machine.
-- swtpm, virtiofsd, passt, and socat for the units.
-- ssh-agent from openssh, for the session agent.
-- ssh-add, ssh-keygen, ssh, and gpg for the tools that flatpak-spawn runs.
-- Remmina, or another client for `rdp://` links, for the Remote Desktop
-  control.
-- pinentry-gnome3 and run0 for the gate and the root setup.
-- virt-fw-vars for the Secure Boot enrollment.
-
-## Notes
-
-- The manifest pins every archive with a sha256 sum. Run `make flatpak-lint` to
-  validate the JSON alone.
-- The build needs the golang and rust-stable SDK extensions.
-- flatpak-builder keeps the downloads, the git mirrors, and the module checksums
-  in .flatpak-builder, so a rerun skips the modules that already finished.
-  Deleting flatpak/build does not lose them.
-- The application module compiles the Blueprint files, the settings schema, and the
-  resource bundle before it builds the binary.
